<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning from Mistakes – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Learning from Mistakes</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn from This Experience?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a short narrative based on a personal experience. Interactive: Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create a narrative describing a lesson learned from a mistake (e.g., "I forgot my homework, so I will make a checklist."). Can provide feedback on a peer's story about school experiences (e.g., "I like how you fixed the problem in your story."). Can create a short paragraph on ways to improve study habits (e.g., "I will study every night.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a picture of a student looking sad next to a messy desk with books open, perhaps implying a missed assignment or poor study habits. Students are asked to write one sentence reflecting on "What went wrong here?" or "What mistake might this student have made?". Then, they write a second sentence about "What could the student learn from this?" or "What will they do next time?". This activates prior knowledge and sets a writing-focused tone.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing two sentences about a simple mistake they made (e.g., "Yesterday, I forgot my keys at home. It was a big mistake.") and what they learned/will do (e.g., "I learned to check my bag carefully. Next time, I will make a checklist."). The teacher points out the use of past simple for the mistake and future tense for the improvement. The teacher also models the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in "-ed" with a focus on /d/ in "improved" and /t/ in "practiced", /ɪd/ in "wanted."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I forgot my homework yesterday,' is that a success or a mistake?" (Expected answer: A mistake.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'Next time, I will study harder,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (100-120 words) from a fictional student's blog post or diary entry, titled "My Worst Science Project and What I Learned." The text will be in a narrative style, describing a challenging experience related to school, a mistake made, and the improvements planned. It will incorporate target vocabulary such as "mistakes," "grades," "study," "habits," "homework," "improvement," "challenging," "learn," "practice," "better," and grammatical structures (Past simple, Future tense, "should").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Linguistic (Grammar: Past simple for experiences, Future tense for improvements, "should" for advice. Vocabulary: nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs related to learning and improvement. Pragmatic: Reflecting on learning, setting goals for improvement. Sociolinguistic: Providing constructive feedback in discussions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the narrative individually. Then, they answer the following questions in writing:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What mistake did the student make in the science project? (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did the student learn from this experience? (Information retrieval)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">List two things the student *will* do to improve next time. (Identify future tense use)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: The student thinks "I should study more to get better grades." (T/F question targeting "should")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using past simple, future tense, and "should" to express experiences, improvements, and advice, incorporating target vocabulary (mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, improvement, better, learn, practice). Students also practice recognizing minimal pair sounds (e.g., /ð/ in "they" versus /d/ in "day") through a quick auditory drill, which can support more accurate spelling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Minimal Pairs Drill (5 min)</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences. Students complete sentences using the correct past simple form of verbs provided in parentheses and relevant vocabulary from a word bank (e.g., "Last week, I _______ (forget) my homework. It was a big _______ (mistake)."). Before beginning, the teacher conducts a quick drill for minimal pairs: "Listen and repeat: 'they' / 'day', 'think' / 'sink', 'van' / 'ban'." Students repeat chorally and individually, focusing on distinguishing the sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Guided Narrative Starters (10 min)</b> Students are given three narrative sentence starters related to a common school mistake (e.g., "One time, I had a challenging exam..." or "I felt bad when I got low grades...") and a list of target vocabulary. They must complete each starter with a sentence describing a mistake, then add another sentence explaining what they learned or what they *will* do to improve, or what they *should* do. This focuses on constructing short coherent narratives. Example: "One time, I had a challenging exam. I didn't study well. I learned to practice every day. Next time, I will prepare carefully."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check & Feedback on Sentences (5 min)</b> Students exchange their completed sentences from activity 2 with a partner. Partners read each other's sentences and use a simple checklist to check for:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Past Simple? (Yes/No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Future Tense or 'should'? (Yes/No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary used? (Yes/No)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">Does it make sense? (Yes/No)</li>
                        </ul>
                        They briefly discuss any feedback, practicing giving advice (e.g., "You should use 'practiced' here, not 'practice'." or "I like how you used the word 'improvement'.").
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Writing and Peer Review Session.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Individual Narrative Writing (15 min):</b> Students will write a short narrative paragraph (5-7 sentences) about a personal school experience where they made a mistake. They must describe the mistake using the past simple, explain what they learned ("I learned...") and outline at least one thing they *will* do differently in the future (using future tense) or what they *should* do (using "should") to improve their study habits or grades. They are encouraged to use at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, improvement, better, successful).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Review and Feedback (10 min):</b> Students exchange their complete narratives with a new partner. Each student reads their partner's narrative and provides constructive feedback based on a rubric or guiding questions focusing on content (did they describe a mistake and a lesson?), grammar (correct use of past simple, future tense, 'should'?), and vocabulary use. They use pragmatic functions like "You should..." or "I like how you..." to give advice and appreciation. For example, "I like how you described your mistake. You should check the past tense of 'ran' here." or "You used 'improvement' well in your story."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during peer review, collection of student narratives for review, and peer assessment checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will submit their final written narrative after incorporating peer feedback. The teacher will also collect the peer feedback forms to assess students' ability to provide constructive feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for narrative):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear description of a personal mistake and what was learned (Clarity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Past Simple to describe the experience.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of Future Tense or "should" to describe future improvements/advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., mistakes, grades, study, habits, improvement).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and intelligibility of the text.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I did well in my story today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I need to improve in my writing for next time is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing your narrative was the most challenging for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did receiving feedback from your partner help you improve your story?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this writing lesson? Students were engaged in identifying mistakes and planning improvements. The peer review session encouraged interactive use of the language and pragmatic functions. The use of past simple and future tense was generally good.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with consistent use of past simple irregular verbs; a quick review or a handout could be beneficial. I noticed difficulty in providing detailed constructive feedback; more explicit modeling of feedback phrases (beyond 'You should...') is needed. I also need to reinforce the pronunciation of "-ed" endings more explicitly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on individual narrative writing and peer feedback. Lesson 5 will build on this by integrating these personal reflections and improvements into a larger 21st Century Skills Project, likely involving mediation (summarizing their learning) and presentation of their improved habits to a wider audience, demonstrating progress and successful strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>